  "pages": [
    {
      "folio": 162,
      "contentBottom": 216.14391276041664,
      "sourceGap": 6.106087239583331,
      "footerGap": 3.5594726562499943,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "GROWING TECHNIQUES - CULTIVATION",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 167.99801432291665,
          "top": 11.997200520833331,
          "bottom": 15.3044921875,
          "fontPt": 7.5
        },
        {
          "tag": "H1",
          "text": "CultivationLifting and using turf",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 167.99801432291665,
          "top": 17.80149739583333,
          "bottom": 29.80283203125,
          "fontPt": 31.5
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "If you are turning a lawn into a vegetable bed and intend to dig it, lift the turf first. It contains useful soil and organic matter, so keep it. You can stack it for later, or bury it as you dig.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 167.99801432291665,
          "top": 32.29983723958333,
          "bottom": 42.29612630208333,
          "fontPt": 10.125
        },
        {
          "tag": "H2",
          "text": "Lift it in manageable pieces",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 49.55563151041666,
          "bottom": 56.19088541666666,
          "fontPt": 16.5
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "Cut the grass short and mark out the bed. With a sharp, square-nosed spade or lawn edging iron, cut strips slightly wider than the spade blade, then cross-cut about twice that width apart.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 58.18766276041666,
          "bottom": 72.52477213541665,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "Start at an edge. Slide the spade under the turf, just below the main mat of roots, and lift each piece. Keep the pieces small enough to handle comfortably. The ground underneath is now ready for digging.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 74.72412109374999,
          "bottom": 93.84026692708332,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "IMG",
          "text": "A spade slides under a thin piece of turf at the edge of a lawn cut into manageable rectangles.",
          "left": 115.99498697916665,
          "right": 167.9938802083333,
          "top": 50.39899088541666,
          "bottom": 88.39563802083332,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "FIGCAPTION",
          "text": "Slice beneath the root mat.",
          "left": 115.99498697916665,
          "right": 167.9938802083333,
          "top": 89.4953125,
          "bottom": 92.99690755208333,
          "fontPt": 7.949999999999999
        },
        {
          "tag": "H2",
          "text": "Stack it for later",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 101.09977213541666,
          "bottom": 107.73502604166666,
          "fontPt": 16.5
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "Make a neat pile with every turf grass-side down. Keep it damp, cover with an opaque tarpaulin and weigh down the edges. Allow at least six months. If it still holds together as turf, leave it longer.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 109.73180338541665,
          "bottom": 124.06891276041665,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "Once it is dark and crumbly, break it up and sieve out stones and any surviving weed roots. This is traditionally called turf loam. Mix it with well-rotted, sieved garden compost for potting, or work it back into a bed.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 126.26826171874998,
          "bottom": 145.38440755208333,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "IMG",
          "text": "A low stack of turves with brown soil uppermost and grass pointing down in every layer. A weighted tarpaulin is folded back to reveal the layers.",
          "left": 115.99498697916665,
          "right": 167.9938802083333,
          "top": 102.44335937499999,
          "bottom": 139.43955078124998,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "FIGCAPTION",
          "text": "Cover folded back to show the layers.",
          "left": 115.99498697916665,
          "right": 167.9938802083333,
          "top": 140.53922526041666,
          "bottom": 144.0408203125,
          "fontPt": 7.949999999999999
        },
        {
          "tag": "H2",
          "text": "Or bury it as you dig",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 154.07845052083331,
          "bottom": 160.71370442708331,
          "fontPt": 16.5
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "If you are double-digging, you can put the turf straight back into the bed. After loosening the subsoil, lay the turves on it grass-side down, then cover with the topsoil from the next trench.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 162.71048177083333,
          "bottom": 177.0475911458333,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "The turf rots beneath the growing layer. Keep the subsoil below the topsoil, as shown in the double-digging steps.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 110.99684244791665,
          "top": 179.24694010416664,
          "bottom": 188.80501302083331,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "svg",
          "text": "title desc",
          "left": 115.99498697916665,
          "right": 167.9938802083333,
          "top": 152.64391276041664,
          "bottom": 185.64241536458331,
          "fontPt": 9.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "FIGCAPTION",
          "text": "Turf goes above the loosened subsoil.",
          "left": 115.99498697916665,
          "right": 167.9938802083333,
          "top": 186.74208984375,
          "bottom": 190.24368489583333,
          "fontPt": 7.949999999999999
        },
        {
          "tag": "H2",
          "text": "Check for persistent weeds",
          "left": 11.997200520833331,
          "right": 164.00032552083331,
          "top": 197.23860677083331,
          "bottom": 202.9684895833333,
          "fontPt": 14.25
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "Remove couch grass runners and other persistent weed roots from the turf and exposed soil. Do not rely on burial or a cool turf stack to kill them.",
          "left": 11.997200520833331,
          "right": 164.00032552083331,
          "top": 204.36582031249998,
          "bottom": 213.14667968749998,
          "fontPt": 9.225000000000001
        },
        {
          "tag": "P",
          "text": "John's Hackriculture manuscript and videos; Garden Organic and RHS. Full sources accompany the draft.",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 167.99801432291665,
          "top": 222.24999999999997,
          "bottom": 225.20175781249998,
          "fontPt": 6.75
        },
        {
          "tag": "FOOTER",
          "text": "GROWING TECHNIQUES\n162",
          "left": 7.999511718749999,
          "right": 167.99801432291665,
          "top": 228.76123046874997,
          "bottom": 231.99824218749998,
          "fontPt": 7.3500000000000005
        }
      ],
      "text": "GROWING TECHNIQUES - CULTIVATION\n\nCultivationLifting and using turf\n\nIf you are turning a lawn into a vegetable bed and intend to dig it, lift the turf first. It contains useful soil and organic matter, so keep it. You can stack it for later, or bury it as you dig.\n\nLift it in manageable pieces\n\nCut the grass short and mark out the bed. With a sharp, square-nosed spade or lawn edging iron, cut strips slightly wider than the spade blade, then cross-cut about twice that width apart.\n\nStart at an edge. Slide the spade under the turf, just below the main mat of roots, and lift each piece. Keep the pieces small enough to handle comfortably. The ground underneath is now ready for digging.\n\nSlice beneath the root mat.\nStack it for later\n\nMake a neat pile with every turf grass-side down. Keep it damp, cover with an opaque tarpaulin and weigh down the edges. Allow at least six months. If it still holds together as turf, leave it longer.\n\nOnce it is dark and crumbly, break it up and sieve out stones and any surviving weed roots. This is traditionally called turf loam. Mix it with well-rotted, sieved garden compost for potting, or work it back into a bed.\n\nCover folded back to show the layers.\nOr bury it as you dig\n\nIf you are double-digging, you can put the turf straight back into the bed. After loosening the subsoil, lay the turves on it grass-side down, then cover with the topsoil from the next trench.\n\nThe turf rots beneath the growing layer. Keep the subsoil below the topsoil, as shown in the double-digging steps.\n\nTopsoil\nTurf, grass-\nside down\nLoosened\nsubsoil\nTurf goes above the loosened subsoil.\nCheck for persistent weeds\n\nRemove couch grass runners and other persistent weed roots from the turf and exposed soil. Do not rely on burial or a cool turf stack to kill them.\n\nJohn's Hackriculture manuscript and videos; Garden Organic and RHS. Full sources accompany the draft.\n\nGROWING TECHNIQUES\n162"
    }
  ],
